NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"issue-1891-cleanup-start-follow-up-oracle-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Issue #1891 cleanup-start follow-up oracle review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed semantic head: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"14b06080e0881f41b5363b8a4a3ffd0c3b3aed31"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Base and prior review-record head:\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"355554078675c9aaf924b649d2bdcd3636e40d4a"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial implementation and control head:\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2fa7fada018613063c2f0d4a915f244ec68412d1"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evaluate Review fix head: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8f0481ad1e5d096a372da7df0002c8d4c2319c9d"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scope: the cleanup-start boundary in core, dependent live-query and Effect\ninvalidation, and the PowerSync late on-demand hook and trigger-disposal repair.\nThe primary executable owner remains\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/collection-cleanup-restart-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":". PowerSync's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/powersync-db-collection/tests/load-hooks.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" is a real-provider\nrefinement. This documentation closeout does not change the reviewed semantic\ntree."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"authority-issue-history-and-selected-contract","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authority, issue history, and selected contract"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Issue #1891 exposed an existing cleanup barrier rather than inventing a new\none. The API and documentation tell callers to await cleanup before starting a\nnew sync run. The minimal reproduction returned a held Promise from a cleanup\ncallback and observed public cleanup settle before that Promise continued. At\nthe time of the report, the callback type and Collection implementation treated\nthe cleanup as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"void"},{"type":"text","value":" and ignored the runtime Promise."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That defect matters even when no row assertion fails immediately. If an old and\nreplacement Collection use the same SQLite resource, old cleanup can affect the\nreplacement or persist deletes after replacement hydration. Without a cleanup\nsettlement barrier, an application needs a separate drain or cancellation\nbarrier before constructing the replacement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two contracts were possible:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Await runtime Promise-like cleanup results before terminal cleanup."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Define cleanup as initiation-only and expose a separate settlement hook."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The project selected the first contract. The initiation-only alternative would\nhave required explicit documentation and another public barrier; silently using\nthose semantics does not satisfy the existing cleanup and restart contract.\nIssue #1889 is adjacent but distinct. It concerns late startup "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markReady"},{"type":"text","value":" and\ninitial-query readiness. Issue #1891 concerns settlement and invalidation of an\nending sync run. Cleanup start is not startup readiness, a Collection status, or\nproof that adapter resources settled."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The earlier repair added awaitable cleanup and preserved these laws:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"concurrent cleanup callers share one Promise;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"adapter rejection ends the old run once, publishes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":", rejects all\nwaiters with the adapter error as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncCleanupError.cause"},{"type":"text","value":", and does not retry\nthe retired callback;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a later sync run remains admissible;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"incidental non-Promise returns from contextual-"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"void"},{"type":"text","value":" callbacks remain\nsynchronous;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"runtime Promise-like returns are awaited even when the callback is statically\ntyped to return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"void"},{"type":"text","value":"; and"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" event admits restart before a continuation on the\npublic cleanup Promise runs."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The previous exact-head review left two PowerSync histories open: an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onLoadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" hook that returns its disposer after cleanup starts, and trigger\ndisposal created by that pending acquisition. Merely delaying terminal\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" until those resources settled was rejected. In the staged-preload\ncounterexample, the dependent live query remained active, accepted the late\nbaseline, and fulfilled preload before source-cleanup publication. The durable\ndesign therefore needed an earlier invalidation boundary distinct from terminal\nstatus."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At the reviewed head, cleanup start synchronously closes restart admission,\nputs each dependent live query in terminal error once, marks dependent Effects\ndisposed, and detaches subscription demand. Throwing and reentrant observers do\nnot strand teardown or observer ownership. The source Collection keeps its\nprior public status while adapter cleanup is pending. PowerSync owns pending\non-demand loads, late hook cleanup, already released cleanup Promises, and\ntrigger disposal until they settle. A pending hook-acquisition failure\nparticipates in cleanup failure selection, while an expected transaction-abort\nconsequence does not replace the primary cleanup failure. Only after all owned\nwork settles does core publish "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" and settle public cleanup."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"oracle-responsibilities","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle responsibilities"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the public cleanup/restart contract, project glossary, and\nlive-query architecture authorize the two boundaries and state their limits."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedCleanupBoundary"},{"type":"text","value":" is an independent two-checkpoint\ntimeline. It predicts restart admission, source status, public cleanup\nsettlement, and a combined dependent-terminal observation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"History grammar:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" fixed histories hold adapter cleanup at cleanup start and\nsettlement. Adjacent bounded cases cover live queries, repeated aliases,\nEffects with pending handlers, abort/release reentry, nested cleanup,\nconcurrent callers, throwing and late observer registration, fulfillment,\nrejection, runtime return shape, late hook return or rejection, already\nreleased cleanup, trigger creation and disposal, and combined cleanup\nfailures."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production driver:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" core calls public Collection, live-query, and Effect\nentry points. PowerSync uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@powersync/node"},{"type":"text","value":" with a real temporary Node\nSQLite database, public source Collections, and dependent live queries."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refinement check:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" held gates define the observation cuts. Tests compare\nexact status, restart admission, Promise identity and settlement, exact error\ncauses and precedence, dependent state, subscriber and observer ownership,\ndisposer and report counts, publications, rows, adapter logs, and the process\nunhandled-rejection channel."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dependent: terminal"},{"type":"text","value":" field is an explicit abstraction. It combines\ntwo different public observations: live-query status "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Effect.disposed === true"},{"type":"text","value":". It does not claim those dependents have the same\ncleanup machinery. Cleanup start maps to the internal synchronous callback\nboundary. Cleanup settlement maps to the later public cleanup Promise cut."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-001-through-orc-011","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 through ORC-011"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001: contract authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Collection.cleanup()"},{"type":"text","value":", the await-before-restart documentation, the glossary's cleanup and cleanup-start terms, and the live-query architecture authorize the result. The exact held-Promise reproduction and same-SQLite replacement hazard establish why settlement matters. The owner remains partial: it does not establish full demand/replay histories, provider transport shutdown, wrapper-wide persistence, general row-publication laws, Effect-handler ordering beyond the pending-disposal case, or native hosts other than the tested Node SQLite path."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002: independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The expected timeline comes from the approved cleanup contract and rejected staged-preload counterexample. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedCleanupBoundary"},{"type":"text","value":" imports no lifecycle transition, callback registry, adapter collector, or Promise classifier. Held gates and sentinel errors compute observations independently of production's cleanup queues and task sets."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The five responsibilities are listed above. The executable owner keeps its contract, limits, pure model, bounded scenarios, public production drivers, recorders, and named cleanup-start and cleanup-settlement checks together. Its repeated-alias, pending-handler, throwing-observer, and late-registration refinements are explicit fixed cases outside the two-checkpoint model. PowerSync keeps provider-specific ownership and failure histories beside its real SQLite driver."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. This follow-up makes no generated-history or input-grammar coverage claim. Its scenario products are bounded enumerations, and the late-hook and trigger tests are fixed controlled schedules. Reconstruction, ablation, random range, and exclusion controls are therefore not triggered."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005: production path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Core reaches public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleanup()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preload()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startSyncImmediate()"},{"type":"text","value":", live-query status, and Effect disposal. It also observes repeated aliases, pending handler settlement, cleanup-start observer counts, exact observer failures, teardown completion, and later restart. PowerSync reaches its public Collection adapter through a real "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PowerSyncDatabase"},{"type":"text","value":" using Node SQLite, a source Collection, and a dependent live query. Positive witnesses include entered hook gates, disposer call counts, source subscriber and observer counts, and trigger creation/disposal. The recorders retain early settlement, wrong status, duplicate terminal reports, wrong error cause or precedence, unexpected publication or adapter logging, and unhandled rejection at the held checkpoints."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006: checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. At the initial implementation head, C-CORE-LIVE-START left a held live query "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loading"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" (1 assertion failure, 18 passes); M-CORE-EFFECT-START observed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"active"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"terminal"},{"type":"text","value":" (1 assertion failure, 19 skipped); M-PS-TRIGGER-AWAIT settled both disposer lanes early (2 assertion failures, 18 skipped); C-PS-LATE-HOOK settled both late-hook lanes early (2 assertion failures, 18 skipped); and C-PS-RELEASED-HOOK settled both released-hook lanes early (2 assertion failures, 20 skipped). Final review controls reproduced duplicate alias reporting ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expected 1"},{"type":"text","value":", received "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2"},{"type":"text","value":"), one leaked late Effect observer ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expected 0"},{"type":"text","value":", received "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":"), and a throwing late observer poisoning the next cleanup. The final in-flight-disposer mutant invoked without awaiting its Promise; both lanes reached the disposer and then failed the pending-settlement assertion (2 failures, 25 skipped). Every temporary mutation and control was restored. None was a timeout, setup failure, or unreached path."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007: fixed and random campaigns with direct replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. No important generated property, generator, seed, run budget, shrink path, or replay interface changed. The bounded and fixed controls do not claim random coverage."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008: stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedCleanupBoundary"},{"type":"text","value":" is stateless recomputation over a prior status and checkpoint. It does not introduce, remove, combine, or split mutable state in a stateful reference model. The distinct cleanup-start and cleanup-settlement inputs preserve the next-action distinction that restart admission exposes."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009: vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleanup start"},{"type":"text","value":", cleanup, restart, sync run, Collection status, settlement, demand, physical acquisition, and acquisition release retain their glossary meanings. The model-only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dependent: terminal"},{"type":"text","value":" abstraction maps explicitly to live-query terminal error or Effect disposed. Cleanup start is an internal invalidation cut, not the terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" status; cleanup settlement is distinct from the terminal event's earlier restart-admission cut. PowerSync's cleanup collector and shared active trigger-disposal Promise express implementation ownership, not new public lifecycle states."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The tests do not shrink or normalize failures. Exact sentinel identity, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncCleanupError.cause"},{"type":"text","value":", settlement order, raw publication arrays, adapter logs, and unhandled-rejection arrays preserve the violated law and checkpoint. Core releases held gates, completes teardown after an observer throws, and cleans dependents in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":". PowerSync attempts all owned work, deterministically prefers trigger-disposal failure over later collector inspection, adopts a hook-acquisition failure without double logging it, and does not promote expected "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncTransactionAbortedError"},{"type":"text","value":" collateral to the primary cleanup failure. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withTestCleanup"},{"type":"text","value":" retains the primary harness failure as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregateError.cause"},{"type":"text","value":" and keeps secondary cleanup errors distinguishable. The mutants were restored before green verification."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011: independent second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The named shared-fault hypothesis is that core or a wrapper starts cleanup but discards a returned Promise or late-owned task. Core's in-memory timeline, the prior persisted same-resource/retired-owner refinement, and PowerSync's real SQLite hook/trigger path use meaningfully different machinery. Repeated-alias live-query reporting, pending Effect-handler disposal, and throwing or reentrant observer registration independently test cleanup-start invalidation and ownership. These formulations share only the settlement, invalidation, once-only release, and primary-failure laws. They make no equivalence claim about row ordering, projection, duplicates, or nonempty results. The same-SQLite multi-process and mobile-host risks remain outside this review."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 is satisfied by this versioned record for semantic head\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"14b06080e0881f41b5363b8a4a3ffd0c3b3aed31"},{"type":"text","value":". It records every ORC-001 through\nORC-011 outcome and is linked from the coverage map. The initial\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2fa7fada018613063c2f0d4a915f244ec68412d1"},{"type":"text","value":" head remains only as provenance for\nthe controls and pre-fix review findings described below."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"calibration-details-and-counterexamples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calibration details and counterexamples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At the initial "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2fa7fada"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation head, the PowerSync trigger mutant\nchanged "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"disposeTrackingAfterAbort()"},{"type":"text","value":" from an owned Promise in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise.allSettled([trackingDisposal, ...pendingLoads])"},{"type":"text","value":" to fire-and-forget.\nBoth fulfillment and rejection lanes reached disposer invocation. While the\ndisposer gate remained held, the public cleanup Promise settled. The restored\nimplementation stayed pending, then fulfilled or rejected with the exact\nsentinel cause after release."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The initial late-hook control held "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onLoadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" before it returned a cleanup\ncallback. Cleanup start had to make the dependent live query terminal\nimmediately while the source status stayed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ready"},{"type":"text","value":". Releasing the hook installed\nits disposer without creating a trigger. Public cleanup remained pending until\nthat disposer settled. The rejection lane produced one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncCleanupError"},{"type":"text","value":" with\nthe sentinel cause and no unhandled rejection. Rows and publication arrays\nstayed empty."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The already released hook control retired the live query first. Its held hook\ncleanup was no longer in the active demand map when Collection cleanup began.\nThe restored adapter adopted that Promise into the cleanup collector. Both\nlanes remained pending, invoked the hook once, and produced no unhandled\nrejection. This distinguishes ownership from mere presence in the current\ndemand map."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The initial core live-query control kept only the terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":"\nlistener. With source cleanup held, the exact cleanup-start checkpoint observed\nlive-query status "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loading"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":". This is the same missing\ndistinction that made the staged-preload counterexample possible."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final core review reproduced three ownership and once-only failures before the\nfixes in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8f0481ad"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"two lexical aliases for one source emitted two terminal error reports instead\nof one;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"an Effect registered during active cleanup disposed immediately but retained\none cleanup-start observer; and"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"an immediately throwing late observer remained registered and rejected the\nnext cleanup."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repaired lifecycle removes a late observer when its immediate registration\ncallback throws. Effect registration releases the callback when cleanup becomes\nactive before the unsubscribe handle can be retained. A fatal live query now\nignores later alias notifications. Focused coverage also holds an Effect handler\nopen while cleanup start disposes the Effect and releases its source\nsubscription, and proves that a throwing observer still allows adapter teardown,\nterminal publication, guard release, and later restart. Omitting cleanup-start\ndelivery from the final core tree produced three intended assertion failures."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final PowerSync review exposed two more failure-loss paths. First,\nreconciliation could claim an in-flight trigger disposer after cleanup took its\nsnapshot. The cleanup-owned call then saw no published disposer and settled\nwithout joining the invocation already in progress. The shared\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activeTrackingDisposal"},{"type":"text","value":" Promise makes both owners join the same settlement.\nThe fulfillment and rejection lanes remain pending through disposer invocation;\na mutant that invoked but did not await the disposer failed both pending checks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Second, rejection directly from a pending "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onLoadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" hook was awaited but\ndiscarded. The active cleanup collector now retains that failure. The first\nbroader repair treated every captured pending-load rejection as a cleanup\nfailure, but the staged-baseline collateral test showed that this incorrectly\npromoted an expected "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncTransactionAbortedError"},{"type":"text","value":". The final repair records the\nhook-acquisition failure at its boundary and leaves expected transaction-abort\ncollateral secondary. A combined probe rejects hook cleanup first and trigger\ndisposal later: cleanup waits for both, deterministically reports the trigger\nerror, emits no duplicate adapter log, and creates no unhandled rejection.\nOrdinary unload remains synchronous and reports a later unadopted hook failure\nexactly once."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two earlier controls remain independent evidence rather than final-head\nmutants. The prior R4 ordering mutant produced\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"adapter cleanup -> public Promise continuation -> cleaned-up event"},{"type":"text","value":"; the core\noracle rejected it because the terminal event must precede the continuation.\nThe terminal-only delay candidate awaited late PowerSync resources but left the\ndependent live query active. Its staged preload fulfilled before cleanup\npublication; the expected outcome was rejection at cleanup start. The assertion\nfailure removed that candidate in favor of cleanup start."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"verification-and-prior-reviewed-head-receipts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verification and prior reviewed-head receipts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At the reviewed head:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the cleanup/restart oracle passed 24 of 24 tests;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"its TypeScript check passed; and"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":" passed before this documentation closeout."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At the Evaluate Review fix head "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8f0481ad"},{"type":"text","value":", before the final behavior-preserving\ndeduplication simplification:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the Effect collateral suite passed 86 of 86 tests;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the Collection subscription-lifecycle collateral passed 445 of 445 tests;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DB TypeScript and targeted ESLint checks passed;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the full PowerSync package passed 170 of 170 tests across 11 files with no\ntype errors;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the PowerSync production build and targeted ESLint check passed; and"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the final no-await in-flight trigger-disposal mutant failed both intended\nassertions and was restored."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The initial "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2fa7fada"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation receipts were 32 of 32 focused core and\nEffect tests, 22 of 22 focused PowerSync load-hook tests, and 165 of 165 full\nPowerSync tests across 11 files, plus the stated DB, DB IVM, PowerSync, lint,\nformat, and diff checks. One earlier PowerSync package run at that stage raced\nits build and could not resolve "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dist/esm/index.d.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"; that was a setup-order\nfailure, and the post-build 165-test rerun was green."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"late-live-query-registration-reconciliation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Late live-query registration reconciliation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed semantic head:\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"889b930170c7c23b75f020297d96fa92f3c31142"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review baseline: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"9cc05782a035ca737e2c3dc360ae4ed417be2045"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A later review found that a live query could start while one source's adapter\ncleanup was pending. Cleanup-start observer registration correctly delivered\nthe active boundary synchronously and put the live query in terminal error, but\nthe builder continued its setup loop. At the baseline, the controlled\nfirst-source history observed one subscription on both the cleaning source and\nthe later healthy source. An intermediate guard stopped later acquisition but\nleft one earlier healthy-source subscription when the cleaning source occupied\nthe second position. The final repair throws the exact terminal setup error\nafter unregistering the current observers. The existing sync-entry rollback\nthen releases all earlier source ownership and prevents later acquisition."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The coverage gap was an inverted lifecycle history. Existing tests created a\ndependent before cleanup began or registered an Effect during active cleanup.\nThey did not start a live query after cleanup start, nor vary the cleaning\nsource's position in a multi-source query. The permanent two-cell refinement\nholds adapter cleanup, places the cleaning source first and second, calls the\npublic live-query preload path, and checks the exact terminal error, one report,\nand zero retained subscriptions on both sources."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001: contract authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The glossary and live-query architecture require cleanup start to put dependent live queries in terminal error. This focused refinement adds the setup-order consequence: setup that observes that terminal boundary retains no partial source ownership. It does not expand the oracle's transport, row-publication, or full demand-history claims."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002: independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. A held adapter-cleanup gate creates the boundary. Exact public status, error text, report count, and source subscriber counts supply the expected observations without importing builder state or transition helpers."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The existing oracle opening retains its contract, two-checkpoint model, fixed history grammar, public driver, and refinement checks. The new fixed source-position matrix is named as an adjacent ownership refinement outside the small timeline model."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The first/second source product is a bounded two-cell enumeration, not a generated-history coverage claim."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005: production path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The driver calls real source "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preload()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleanup()"},{"type":"text","value":", creates a real joined live-query Collection, and calls its public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preload()"},{"type":"text","value":". Both cells reach synchronous cleanup-start delivery while adapter cleanup remains held. The checkpoint observes terminal status and exact subscriber ownership before cleanup settlement."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006: checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. At the baseline, the first-source cell failed with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ cleaning: 1, healthy: 1 }"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of zeros. The intermediate stop-only guard made that cell pass but the second-source cell failed with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ cleaning: 0, healthy: 1 }"},{"type":"text","value":". Neither result was a timeout, setup failure, or unreached path. The final rollback repair passes both cells."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007: fixed and random campaigns with direct replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. No important generated property, seed, budget, shrink path, or replay interface changed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008: stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. No stateful reference-model state was added, removed, combined, or split. Source position is a fixed driver dimension."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009: vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Cleanup start, cleanup settlement, live-query Collection, source Collection, subscription, and sync run retain their glossary meanings. Partial source ownership means subscriptions acquired before terminal setup rollback; it is an observation, not a new lifecycle state."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The test preserves exact status, error, log, and subscriber-count observations. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":" releases the adapter gate, cleans the live query and both sources, and cannot replace the primary assertion failure."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011: independent second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The existing late-Effect-registration refinement independently proves immediate disposal and observer release. The new live-query path instead exercises joined-source setup rollback and subscription ownership. Both refine cleanup-start invalidation without equating their teardown machinery."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 is satisfied by this versioned section for semantic head\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"889b930170c7c23b75f020297d96fa92f3c31142"},{"type":"text","value":". Focused verification passed the\n30-test cleanup/restart oracle, the 12-test Effect-disposal oracle, and the\n199-test Collection subscription-lifecycle oracle. The DB build, DB TypeScript\ncheck, targeted ESLint and Prettier checks, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":" also passed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ancestor record\n"},{"type":"link","href":"issue-1891-async-cleanup.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"issue-1891-async-cleanup.md"}]},{"type":"text","value":" reviewed semantic\nhead "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"21ed924352e91e47f0643de6d2542051ddd12d20"},{"type":"text","value":" and was versioned by\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"355554078675c9aaf924b649d2bdcd3636e40d4a"},{"type":"text","value":". Its receipts were 349 core tests,\n292 persistence tests with one existing todo, and 154 PowerSync tests, plus the\nstated type, lint, format, and build checks. That record classified pending late\nhooks and trigger disposal as an open design gap and named cleanup start only as\nthe destination. This follow-up implements and calibrates that destination; it\ndoes not rewrite the earlier verdict."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"design-grammar-reconciliation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Design-grammar reconciliation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed semantic head:\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"20407a501a2441cd3780198809e75c00eb410925"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The frozen preservation contract required the model to retain cleanup start,\nall-owned retirement, observer and adapter failure aggregation, late-registration\nrollback, one notification per owner per boundary, terminal dependent ownership\nacross source restart, and the PowerSync pending-resource refinement. The\nsmallest reconstruction is a graph of independent laws rather than one model of\nevery lifecycle manager:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a three-cut timeline owns cleanup start, terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" publication,\nand later public cleanup-Promise settlement;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"observer registration ownership crosses cleanup-boundary identity, so one\nregistration receives one notification for each boundary until it\nunsubscribes;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"dependent ownership crosses sync-run identity, so source restart does not\nrevive a live query terminalized by the retired run;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"setup rollback owns the inverted history in which a dependent registers while\ncleanup is already active;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a provenance-labelled failure relation keeps adapter failure primary and\nobserver or local failure secondary; and"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the PowerSync task collector refines the one adapter-cleanup obligation with\nactive, released, pending, and late hook cleanup plus present or in-flight\ntrigger disposal."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core timeline now models all three observable cuts. At cleanup start,\nrestart admission is closed, public status is unchanged, and cleanup is\npending. At terminal publication, admission is open and status is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":"\nwhile the original cleanup Promise remains pending. Promise settlement follows.\nThe model still does not reproduce callback registries, manager order, provider\ntasks, replay, or publication machinery."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reconstruction-ablation-exclusion-and-range","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconstruction, ablation, exclusion, and range"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every known valid witness reconstructs from the graph above. The timeline owns\nthe held-adapter case. Separate driver refinements cover live queries, Effects,\nrepeated aliases, late observers, source position, abort and release reentry,\nruntime cleanup return shape, concurrent callers, terminal-event restart, and\nfailure combinations. PowerSync separately covers each provider task origin and\ntheir consequential overlap."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One-at-a-time ablation produced these results:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Removed coordinate or relation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lost distinction"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cleanup start"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dependents remain active while adapter cleanup is pending."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Terminal-publication cut"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Event-time restart and event-before-Promise order disappear."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cleanup-Promise cut"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapter settlement becomes indistinguishable from public completion."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sync-run identity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A source restart may revive an already-terminal dependent."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registration by cleanup boundary"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once-per-boundary and once-per-lifetime become indistinguishable."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cleaning-source position"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Setup can retain an owner acquired before the cleaning source."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapter/local failure provenance"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rejection timing may choose the wrong primary failure."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime return shape"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A contextual-"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"void"},{"type":"text","value":" Promise can be mistaken for synchronous cleanup."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PowerSync task origin"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A late hook, released hook, or in-flight trigger can fall outside adapter ownership."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PowerSync task-set growth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cleanup can snapshot tasks once and settle before a late disposer."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two-attempt reentry coordinate does not justify a full Cartesian expansion\nby itself, but it retains one repeated-denial observation without meaningful\nruntime cost. The existing eight reentry cells therefore remain. Stable case\nIDs now calibrate the complete "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"abort|release × plain|nested × 1|2"},{"type":"text","value":" product and\nthe complete "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event|await × source|live-query"},{"type":"text","value":" product. The executable check\nasserts the expected order, completeness, and uniqueness of all twelve IDs.\nThe source-position matrix and PowerSync result-shape matrices are explicit\none-dimensional enumerations with no derived product or skipped cells."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nearby invalid states are rejected or kept outside this owner:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"an observer registered after cleanup settlement does not retroactively\nobserve the old boundary;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"restarting or recreating a live-query Collection differs from restarting\nonly its cleaned source;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a settled ordinary-unload failure is reported once rather than retroactively\nadopted by a later cleanup; and"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"provider transport shutdown, demand replay, and general row publication\nremain outside this oracle."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No independent marginal case was supplied after the grammar froze. Range is\ntherefore untested beyond the current core driver and the Node SQLite PowerSync\nrefinement. Existing persistence and PowerSync cases shaped the grammar and are\nnot held-out range evidence. Browser, mobile, multi-process, and provider\ntransport behavior remain outside the claim."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"new-executable-controls","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New executable controls"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core owner adds three focused witnesses:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A cleanup-start observer throws before the adapter cleanup later rejects.\nThe final "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregateError"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps the wrapped adapter failure as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cause"},{"type":"text","value":" and\nthe observer failure as its secondary error."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One ordinary observer remains registered across two cleanup/restart\nboundaries, receives exactly one notification for each, then receives none\nafter unsubscribe."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Restarting a cleaned source leaves the original dependent live query in\nterminal error, preserves its prior result, and emits no replacement\npublication."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The PowerSync refinement adds the higher-order overlap omitted by the separate\nlate-hook and in-flight-trigger cases. One cleanup starts while trigger creation\nand another subset hook are both pending. Both later cleanup functions are\nowned and invoked once. Hook cleanup rejects first while trigger disposal stays\npending. Cleanup remains pending, then rejects with trigger disposal as the\ndeterministic primary failure, without duplicate adapter logging or an\nunhandled rejection."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The direct observer-plus-adapter checker was calibrated with a temporary\npriority-inversion mutant. The mutant made the observer failure the aggregate\ncause and failed the intended test at its exact cause assertion: one failure and\n33 skipped tests. Restoring production made all 34 core oracle tests pass. No\nproduction change was required."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reusable-boundary-law-prompts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reusable boundary-law prompts"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prompt"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disposition"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Real-provider conformance"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable to the provider refinement. PowerSync runs through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@powersync/node"},{"type":"text","value":" and a real temporary Node SQLite database. The in-memory core fixture makes no provider-value claim."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Minimal ambiguity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable and covered. Incidental non-Promise return, runtime Promise, synchronous throw, asynchronous rejection, and fulfillment have separate witnesses."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Name invariance"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. No user-controlled name or SQL alias selects cleanup classification."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Representation symmetry"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable only to cleanup return representation. Function and object cleanup forms plus contextual-"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"void"},{"type":"text","value":" runtime Promises preserve the same settlement law."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Await-boundary transitions"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable and covered at cleanup start, terminal publication, adapter settlement, late hook return, trigger creation, and disposer settlement."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local/transport refinement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boundary only. Core and Node SQLite share lifecycle settlement; provider transport termination remains explicitly unclaimed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partial-construction cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable and covered for cleaning-source position, pending hook acquisition, trigger creation, and their combined overlap. This does not claim every provider construction step."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Value-and-work refinement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable as a bounded-work law. Exact once counts protect ownership, not performance or transfer cardinality."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"exact-head-environment-and-receipts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exact-head environment and receipts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The semantic head ran with Node "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"24.19.0"},{"type":"text","value":", pnpm "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"11.1.0"},{"type":"text","value":", Vitest "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"3.2.4"},{"type":"text","value":",\nTypeScript "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"5.9.3"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@powersync/node"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0.20.0"},{"type":"text","value":", and Darwin "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"25.6.0"},{"type":"text","value":" on arm64."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The cleanup/restart oracle passed 34 of 34 tests with no type errors."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The PowerSync load-hook suite passed 33 of 33 tests with no type errors."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The full PowerSync package passed 176 of 176 tests across 11 files with no\ntype errors."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Targeted ESLint and Prettier checks passed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":" passed."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first full DB-package verification executed 6,406 assertions across 216\nfiles but the type checker rejected the event recorder's broad\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CollectionStatus"},{"type":"text","value":" annotation. The follow-up semantic head above narrows that\nobservation from the already-discriminated event value. The targeted oracle and\nits type check then passed. The final full DB-package rerun passed all 6,406\ntests across 216 files with no type errors."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"multiple-cleanup-start-failure-reconciliation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multiple cleanup-start failure reconciliation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed semantic head:\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eb93f42094a365f5b2b599504ed4ffea4da66bbb"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review baseline: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"7de64caeeb46c0a6d2b9f5c7e7cf424d7e6193da"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A final current-head review found one failure-fidelity gap. Cleanup start\nattempted every observer, but "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runAllCallbacks"},{"type":"text","value":" rethrew only the first observer\nfailure and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"beginCleanup"},{"type":"text","value":" retained only one local failure. With two throwing\nobservers and a rejecting adapter cleanup, the public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregateError"},{"type":"text","value":" retained\nthe wrapped adapter failure and the first observer failure but silently dropped\nthe second observer failure."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fixed oracle is the smallest distinguishing history: two cleanup-start\nobservers throw distinct sentinel errors in registration order, adapter cleanup\nrejects after a held gate, and cleanup then admits a new sync run. The RED run\nreached both observers and failed because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregateError.errors"},{"type":"text","value":" had two entries\ninstead of three. Production now records every local teardown failure in\nexecution order. Adapter cleanup remains the primary "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cause"},{"type":"text","value":"; a lone local\nfailure keeps its identity; multiple local-only failures use the first local\nfailure as their aggregate cause. Teardown, terminal publication, and later\nrestart still complete."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconciliation outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The existing cleanup contract and ORC-010 failure-fidelity law require each cleanup diagnostic to remain distinguishable. The test does not claim provider transport shutdown or full demand/replay coverage."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Expected error identity and order come from callback execution order and adapter-primary precedence, not the production aggregator."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The existing contract, timeline model, fixed failure history, public Collection driver, and exact aggregate comparison remain distinguishable."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. This is one fixed controlled history, not a generated-grammar claim."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The test calls public preload, cleanup, and restart entry points; observer call order, exact error identities, status, and restart are observed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Baseline production was the hostile wrong design. It reached both callbacks and failed only the missing-third-error assertion."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. No important generated property changed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. No stateful reference model changed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Cleanup start, adapter cleanup, local teardown, settlement, and restart retain their glossary meanings."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The aggregate keeps the adapter failure as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cause"},{"type":"text","value":" and retains both observer failures in execution order while cleanup releases resources and permits restart."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The named defect is information loss in one concrete aggregator; no meaningfully independent semantic formulation is needed beyond exact sentinel identities and order."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 is satisfied for this repair by the requirement-by-requirement record\nabove. The focused RED run failed one of one selected tests with no type errors.\nAfter the repair, the complete cleanup/restart oracle passed 37 of 37 tests,\nand the five-suite lifecycle collateral passed 204 of 204 tests with no type\nerrors. Targeted ESLint, Prettier, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":" passed."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"issue-1891-cleanup-start-follow-up-oracle-review","text":"Issue #1891 cleanup-start follow-up oracle review","level":1},{"id":"authority-issue-history-and-selected-contract","text":"Authority, issue history, and selected contract","level":2},{"id":"oracle-responsibilities","text":"Oracle responsibilities","level":2},{"id":"orc-001-through-orc-011","text":"ORC-001 through ORC-011","level":2},{"id":"calibration-details-and-counterexamples","text":"Calibration details and counterexamples","level":2},{"id":"verification-and-prior-reviewed-head-receipts","text":"Verification and prior reviewed-head receipts","level":2},{"id":"late-live-query-registration-reconciliation","text":"Late live-query registration reconciliation","level":2},{"id":"design-grammar-reconciliation","text":"Design-grammar reconciliation","level":2},{"id":"reconstruction-ablation-exclusion-and-range","text":"Reconstruction, ablation, exclusion, and range","level":3},{"id":"new-executable-controls","text":"New executable controls","level":3},{"id":"reusable-boundary-law-prompts","text":"Reusable boundary-law prompts","level":3},{"id":"exact-head-environment-and-receipts","text":"Exact-head environment and receipts","level":3},{"id":"multiple-cleanup-start-failure-reconciliation","text":"Multiple cleanup-start failure reconciliation","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Issue #1891 cleanup-start follow-up oracle review\n\nReviewed semantic head: `14b06080e0881f41b5363b8a4a3ffd0c3b3aed31`\n\nBase and prior review-record head:\n`355554078675c9aaf924b649d2bdcd3636e40d4a`\n\nInitial implementation and control head:\n`2fa7fada018613063c2f0d4a915f244ec68412d1`\n\nEvaluate Review fix head: `8f0481ad1e5d096a372da7df0002c8d4c2319c9d`\n\nScope: the cleanup-start boundary in core, dependent live-query and Effect\ninvalidation, and the PowerSync late on-demand hook and trigger-disposal repair.\nThe primary executable owner remains\n`packages/db/tests/collection-cleanup-restart-oracle.test.ts`. PowerSync's\n`packages/powersync-db-collection/tests/load-hooks.test.ts` is a real-provider\nrefinement. This documentation closeout does not change the reviewed semantic\ntree.\n\n## Authority, issue history, and selected contract\n\nIssue #1891 exposed an existing cleanup barrier rather than inventing a new\none. The API and documentation tell callers to await cleanup before starting a\nnew sync run. The minimal reproduction returned a held Promise from a cleanup\ncallback and observed public cleanup settle before that Promise continued. At\nthe time of the report, the callback type and Collection implementation treated\nthe cleanup as `void` and ignored the runtime Promise.\n\nThat defect matters even when no row assertion fails immediately. If an old and\nreplacement Collection use the same SQLite resource, old cleanup can affect the\nreplacement or persist deletes after replacement hydration. Without a cleanup\nsettlement barrier, an application needs a separate drain or cancellation\nbarrier before constructing the replacement.\n\nTwo contracts were possible:\n\n1. Await runtime Promise-like cleanup results before terminal cleanup.\n2. Define cleanup as initiation-only and expose a separate settlement hook.\n\nThe project selected the first contract. The initiation-only alternative would\nhave required explicit documentation and another public barrier; silently using\nthose semantics does not satisfy the existing cleanup and restart contract.\nIssue #1889 is adjacent but distinct. It concerns late startup `markReady` and\ninitial-query readiness. Issue #1891 concerns settlement and invalidation of an\nending sync run. Cleanup start is not startup readiness, a Collection status, or\nproof that adapter resources settled.\n\nThe earlier repair added awaitable cleanup and preserved these laws:\n\n- concurrent cleanup callers share one Promise;\n- adapter rejection ends the old run once, publishes `cleaned-up`, rejects all\n  waiters with the adapter error as `SyncCleanupError.cause`, and does not retry\n  the retired callback;\n- a later sync run remains admissible;\n- incidental non-Promise returns from contextual-`void` callbacks remain\n  synchronous;\n- runtime Promise-like returns are awaited even when the callback is statically\n  typed to return `void`; and\n- the terminal `cleaned-up` event admits restart before a continuation on the\n  public cleanup Promise runs.\n\nThe previous exact-head review left two PowerSync histories open: an\n`onLoadSubset` hook that returns its disposer after cleanup starts, and trigger\ndisposal created by that pending acquisition. Merely delaying terminal\n`cleaned-up` until those resources settled was rejected. In the staged-preload\ncounterexample, the dependent live query remained active, accepted the late\nbaseline, and fulfilled preload before source-cleanup publication. The durable\ndesign therefore needed an earlier invalidation boundary distinct from terminal\nstatus.\n\nAt the reviewed head, cleanup start synchronously closes restart admission,\nputs each dependent live query in terminal error once, marks dependent Effects\ndisposed, and detaches subscription demand. Throwing and reentrant observers do\nnot strand teardown or observer ownership. The source Collection keeps its\nprior public status while adapter cleanup is pending. PowerSync owns pending\non-demand loads, late hook cleanup, already released cleanup Promises, and\ntrigger disposal until they settle. A pending hook-acquisition failure\nparticipates in cleanup failure selection, while an expected transaction-abort\nconsequence does not replace the primary cleanup failure. Only after all owned\nwork settles does core publish `cleaned-up` and settle public cleanup.\n\n## Oracle responsibilities\n\n- **Contract:** the public cleanup/restart contract, project glossary, and\n  live-query architecture authorize the two boundaries and state their limits.\n- **Model:** `expectedCleanupBoundary` is an independent two-checkpoint\n  timeline. It predicts restart admission, source status, public cleanup\n  settlement, and a combined dependent-terminal observation.\n- **History grammar:** fixed histories hold adapter cleanup at cleanup start and\n  settlement. Adjacent bounded cases cover live queries, repeated aliases,\n  Effects with pending handlers, abort/release reentry, nested cleanup,\n  concurrent callers, throwing and late observer registration, fulfillment,\n  rejection, runtime return shape, late hook return or rejection, already\n  released cleanup, trigger creation and disposal, and combined cleanup\n  failures.\n- **Production driver:** core calls public Collection, live-query, and Effect\n  entry points. PowerSync uses `@powersync/node` with a real temporary Node\n  SQLite database, public source Collections, and dependent live queries.\n- **Refinement check:** held gates define the observation cuts. Tests compare\n  exact status, restart admission, Promise identity and settlement, exact error\n  causes and precedence, dependent state, subscriber and observer ownership,\n  disposer and report counts, publications, rows, adapter logs, and the process\n  unhandled-rejection channel.\n\nThe model's `dependent: terminal` field is an explicit abstraction. It combines\ntwo different public observations: live-query status `error` and\n`Effect.disposed === true`. It does not claim those dependents have the same\ncleanup machinery. Cleanup start maps to the internal synchronous callback\nboundary. Cleanup settlement maps to the later public cleanup Promise cut.\n\n## ORC-001 through ORC-011\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001: contract authority and limits | Pass. `Collection.cleanup()`, the await-before-restart documentation, the glossary's cleanup and cleanup-start terms, and the live-query architecture authorize the result. The exact held-Promise reproduction and same-SQLite replacement hazard establish why settlement matters. The owner remains partial: it does not establish full demand/replay histories, provider transport shutdown, wrapper-wide persistence, general row-publication laws, Effect-handler ordering beyond the pending-disposal case, or native hosts other than the tested Node SQLite path. |\n| ORC-002: independent judgment | Pass. The expected timeline comes from the approved cleanup contract and rejected staged-preload counterexample. `expectedCleanupBoundary` imports no lifecycle transition, callback registry, adapter collector, or Promise classifier. Held gates and sentinel errors compute observations independently of production's cleanup queues and task sets. |\n| ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. The five responsibilities are listed above. The executable owner keeps its contract, limits, pure model, bounded scenarios, public production drivers, recorders, and named cleanup-start and cleanup-settlement checks together. Its repeated-alias, pending-handler, throwing-observer, and late-registration refinements are explicit fixed cases outside the two-checkpoint model. PowerSync keeps provider-specific ownership and failure histories beside its real SQLite driver. |\n| ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls | Not applicable. This follow-up makes no generated-history or input-grammar coverage claim. Its scenario products are bounded enumerations, and the late-hook and trigger tests are fixed controlled schedules. Reconstruction, ablation, random range, and exclusion controls are therefore not triggered. |\n| ORC-005: production path and observation | Pass. Core reaches public `cleanup()`, `preload()`, `startSyncImmediate()`, live-query status, and Effect disposal. It also observes repeated aliases, pending handler settlement, cleanup-start observer counts, exact observer failures, teardown completion, and later restart. PowerSync reaches its public Collection adapter through a real `PowerSyncDatabase` using Node SQLite, a source Collection, and a dependent live query. Positive witnesses include entered hook gates, disposer call counts, source subscriber and observer counts, and trigger creation/disposal. The recorders retain early settlement, wrong status, duplicate terminal reports, wrong error cause or precedence, unexpected publication or adapter logging, and unhandled rejection at the held checkpoints. |\n| ORC-006: checker calibration | Pass. At the initial implementation head, C-CORE-LIVE-START left a held live query `loading` instead of `error` (1 assertion failure, 18 passes); M-CORE-EFFECT-START observed `active` instead of `terminal` (1 assertion failure, 19 skipped); M-PS-TRIGGER-AWAIT settled both disposer lanes early (2 assertion failures, 18 skipped); C-PS-LATE-HOOK settled both late-hook lanes early (2 assertion failures, 18 skipped); and C-PS-RELEASED-HOOK settled both released-hook lanes early (2 assertion failures, 20 skipped). Final review controls reproduced duplicate alias reporting (`expected 1`, received `2`), one leaked late Effect observer (`expected 0`, received `1`), and a throwing late observer poisoning the next cleanup. The final in-flight-disposer mutant invoked without awaiting its Promise; both lanes reached the disposer and then failed the pending-settlement assertion (2 failures, 25 skipped). Every temporary mutation and control was restored. None was a timeout, setup failure, or unreached path. |\n| ORC-007: fixed and random campaigns with direct replay | Not applicable. No important generated property, generator, seed, run budget, shrink path, or replay interface changed. The bounded and fixed controls do not claim random coverage. |\n| ORC-008: stateful-model minimality | Not applicable. `expectedCleanupBoundary` is stateless recomputation over a prior status and checkpoint. It does not introduce, remove, combine, or split mutable state in a stateful reference model. The distinct cleanup-start and cleanup-settlement inputs preserve the next-action distinction that restart admission exposes. |\n| ORC-009: vocabulary mapping | Pass. `cleanup start`, cleanup, restart, sync run, Collection status, settlement, demand, physical acquisition, and acquisition release retain their glossary meanings. The model-only `dependent: terminal` abstraction maps explicitly to live-query terminal error or Effect disposed. Cleanup start is an internal invalidation cut, not the terminal `cleaned-up` status; cleanup settlement is distinct from the terminal event's earlier restart-admission cut. PowerSync's cleanup collector and shared active trigger-disposal Promise express implementation ownership, not new public lifecycle states. |\n| ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass. The tests do not shrink or normalize failures. Exact sentinel identity, `SyncCleanupError.cause`, settlement order, raw publication arrays, adapter logs, and unhandled-rejection arrays preserve the violated law and checkpoint. Core releases held gates, completes teardown after an observer throws, and cleans dependents in `finally`. PowerSync attempts all owned work, deterministically prefers trigger-disposal failure over later collector inspection, adopts a hook-acquisition failure without double logging it, and does not promote expected `SyncTransactionAbortedError` collateral to the primary cleanup failure. `withTestCleanup` retains the primary harness failure as `AggregateError.cause` and keeps secondary cleanup errors distinguishable. The mutants were restored before green verification. |\n| ORC-011: independent second formulation | Pass. The named shared-fault hypothesis is that core or a wrapper starts cleanup but discards a returned Promise or late-owned task. Core's in-memory timeline, the prior persisted same-resource/retired-owner refinement, and PowerSync's real SQLite hook/trigger path use meaningfully different machinery. Repeated-alias live-query reporting, pending Effect-handler disposal, and throwing or reentrant observer registration independently test cleanup-start invalidation and ownership. These formulations share only the settlement, invalidation, once-only release, and primary-failure laws. They make no equivalence claim about row ordering, projection, duplicates, or nonempty results. The same-SQLite multi-process and mobile-host risks remain outside this review. |\n\nORC-012 is satisfied by this versioned record for semantic head\n`14b06080e0881f41b5363b8a4a3ffd0c3b3aed31`. It records every ORC-001 through\nORC-011 outcome and is linked from the coverage map. The initial\n`2fa7fada018613063c2f0d4a915f244ec68412d1` head remains only as provenance for\nthe controls and pre-fix review findings described below.\n\n## Calibration details and counterexamples\n\nAt the initial `2fa7fada` implementation head, the PowerSync trigger mutant\nchanged `disposeTrackingAfterAbort()` from an owned Promise in\n`Promise.allSettled([trackingDisposal, ...pendingLoads])` to fire-and-forget.\nBoth fulfillment and rejection lanes reached disposer invocation. While the\ndisposer gate remained held, the public cleanup Promise settled. The restored\nimplementation stayed pending, then fulfilled or rejected with the exact\nsentinel cause after release.\n\nThe initial late-hook control held `onLoadSubset` before it returned a cleanup\ncallback. Cleanup start had to make the dependent live query terminal\nimmediately while the source status stayed `ready`. Releasing the hook installed\nits disposer without creating a trigger. Public cleanup remained pending until\nthat disposer settled. The rejection lane produced one `SyncCleanupError` with\nthe sentinel cause and no unhandled rejection. Rows and publication arrays\nstayed empty.\n\nThe already released hook control retired the live query first. Its held hook\ncleanup was no longer in the active demand map when Collection cleanup began.\nThe restored adapter adopted that Promise into the cleanup collector. Both\nlanes remained pending, invoked the hook once, and produced no unhandled\nrejection. This distinguishes ownership from mere presence in the current\ndemand map.\n\nThe initial core live-query control kept only the terminal `cleaned-up`\nlistener. With source cleanup held, the exact cleanup-start checkpoint observed\nlive-query status `loading` instead of `error`. This is the same missing\ndistinction that made the staged-preload counterexample possible.\n\nFinal core review reproduced three ownership and once-only failures before the\nfixes in `8f0481ad`:\n\n- two lexical aliases for one source emitted two terminal error reports instead\n  of one;\n- an Effect registered during active cleanup disposed immediately but retained\n  one cleanup-start observer; and\n- an immediately throwing late observer remained registered and rejected the\n  next cleanup.\n\nThe repaired lifecycle removes a late observer when its immediate registration\ncallback throws. Effect registration releases the callback when cleanup becomes\nactive before the unsubscribe handle can be retained. A fatal live query now\nignores later alias notifications. Focused coverage also holds an Effect handler\nopen while cleanup start disposes the Effect and releases its source\nsubscription, and proves that a throwing observer still allows adapter teardown,\nterminal publication, guard release, and later restart. Omitting cleanup-start\ndelivery from the final core tree produced three intended assertion failures.\n\nFinal PowerSync review exposed two more failure-loss paths. First,\nreconciliation could claim an in-flight trigger disposer after cleanup took its\nsnapshot. The cleanup-owned call then saw no published disposer and settled\nwithout joining the invocation already in progress. The shared\n`activeTrackingDisposal` Promise makes both owners join the same settlement.\nThe fulfillment and rejection lanes remain pending through disposer invocation;\na mutant that invoked but did not await the disposer failed both pending checks.\n\nSecond, rejection directly from a pending `onLoadSubset` hook was awaited but\ndiscarded. The active cleanup collector now retains that failure. The first\nbroader repair treated every captured pending-load rejection as a cleanup\nfailure, but the staged-baseline collateral test showed that this incorrectly\npromoted an expected `SyncTransactionAbortedError`. The final repair records the\nhook-acquisition failure at its boundary and leaves expected transaction-abort\ncollateral secondary. A combined probe rejects hook cleanup first and trigger\ndisposal later: cleanup waits for both, deterministically reports the trigger\nerror, emits no duplicate adapter log, and creates no unhandled rejection.\nOrdinary unload remains synchronous and reports a later unadopted hook failure\nexactly once.\n\nTwo earlier controls remain independent evidence rather than final-head\nmutants. The prior R4 ordering mutant produced\n`adapter cleanup -> public Promise continuation -> cleaned-up event`; the core\noracle rejected it because the terminal event must precede the continuation.\nThe terminal-only delay candidate awaited late PowerSync resources but left the\ndependent live query active. Its staged preload fulfilled before cleanup\npublication; the expected outcome was rejection at cleanup start. The assertion\nfailure removed that candidate in favor of cleanup start.\n\n## Verification and prior reviewed-head receipts\n\nAt the reviewed head:\n\n- the cleanup/restart oracle passed 24 of 24 tests;\n- its TypeScript check passed; and\n- `git diff --check` passed before this documentation closeout.\n\nAt the Evaluate Review fix head `8f0481ad`, before the final behavior-preserving\ndeduplication simplification:\n\n- the Effect collateral suite passed 86 of 86 tests;\n- the Collection subscription-lifecycle collateral passed 445 of 445 tests;\n- DB TypeScript and targeted ESLint checks passed;\n- the full PowerSync package passed 170 of 170 tests across 11 files with no\n  type errors;\n- the PowerSync production build and targeted ESLint check passed; and\n- the final no-await in-flight trigger-disposal mutant failed both intended\n  assertions and was restored.\n\nThe initial `2fa7fada` implementation receipts were 32 of 32 focused core and\nEffect tests, 22 of 22 focused PowerSync load-hook tests, and 165 of 165 full\nPowerSync tests across 11 files, plus the stated DB, DB IVM, PowerSync, lint,\nformat, and diff checks. One earlier PowerSync package run at that stage raced\nits build and could not resolve `dist/esm/index.d.ts`; that was a setup-order\nfailure, and the post-build 165-test rerun was green.\n\n## Late live-query registration reconciliation\n\nReviewed semantic head:\n`889b930170c7c23b75f020297d96fa92f3c31142`\n\nReview baseline: `9cc05782a035ca737e2c3dc360ae4ed417be2045`\n\nA later review found that a live query could start while one source's adapter\ncleanup was pending. Cleanup-start observer registration correctly delivered\nthe active boundary synchronously and put the live query in terminal error, but\nthe builder continued its setup loop. At the baseline, the controlled\nfirst-source history observed one subscription on both the cleaning source and\nthe later healthy source. An intermediate guard stopped later acquisition but\nleft one earlier healthy-source subscription when the cleaning source occupied\nthe second position. The final repair throws the exact terminal setup error\nafter unregistering the current observers. The existing sync-entry rollback\nthen releases all earlier source ownership and prevents later acquisition.\n\nThe coverage gap was an inverted lifecycle history. Existing tests created a\ndependent before cleanup began or registered an Effect during active cleanup.\nThey did not start a live query after cleanup start, nor vary the cleaning\nsource's position in a multi-source query. The permanent two-cell refinement\nholds adapter cleanup, places the cleaning source first and second, calls the\npublic live-query preload path, and checks the exact terminal error, one report,\nand zero retained subscriptions on both sources.\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001: contract authority and limits | Pass. The glossary and live-query architecture require cleanup start to put dependent live queries in terminal error. This focused refinement adds the setup-order consequence: setup that observes that terminal boundary retains no partial source ownership. It does not expand the oracle's transport, row-publication, or full demand-history claims. |\n| ORC-002: independent judgment | Pass. A held adapter-cleanup gate creates the boundary. Exact public status, error text, report count, and source subscriber counts supply the expected observations without importing builder state or transition helpers. |\n| ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. The existing oracle opening retains its contract, two-checkpoint model, fixed history grammar, public driver, and refinement checks. The new fixed source-position matrix is named as an adjacent ownership refinement outside the small timeline model. |\n| ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls | Not applicable. The first/second source product is a bounded two-cell enumeration, not a generated-history coverage claim. |\n| ORC-005: production path and observation | Pass. The driver calls real source `preload()` and `cleanup()`, creates a real joined live-query Collection, and calls its public `preload()`. Both cells reach synchronous cleanup-start delivery while adapter cleanup remains held. The checkpoint observes terminal status and exact subscriber ownership before cleanup settlement. |\n| ORC-006: checker calibration | Pass. At the baseline, the first-source cell failed with `{ cleaning: 1, healthy: 1 }` instead of zeros. The intermediate stop-only guard made that cell pass but the second-source cell failed with `{ cleaning: 0, healthy: 1 }`. Neither result was a timeout, setup failure, or unreached path. The final rollback repair passes both cells. |\n| ORC-007: fixed and random campaigns with direct replay | Not applicable. No important generated property, seed, budget, shrink path, or replay interface changed. |\n| ORC-008: stateful-model minimality | Not applicable. No stateful reference-model state was added, removed, combined, or split. Source position is a fixed driver dimension. |\n| ORC-009: vocabulary mapping | Pass. Cleanup start, cleanup settlement, live-query Collection, source Collection, subscription, and sync run retain their glossary meanings. Partial source ownership means subscriptions acquired before terminal setup rollback; it is an observation, not a new lifecycle state. |\n| ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass. The test preserves exact status, error, log, and subscriber-count observations. `finally` releases the adapter gate, cleans the live query and both sources, and cannot replace the primary assertion failure. |\n| ORC-011: independent second formulation | Pass. The existing late-Effect-registration refinement independently proves immediate disposal and observer release. The new live-query path instead exercises joined-source setup rollback and subscription ownership. Both refine cleanup-start invalidation without equating their teardown machinery. |\n\nORC-012 is satisfied by this versioned section for semantic head\n`889b930170c7c23b75f020297d96fa92f3c31142`. Focused verification passed the\n30-test cleanup/restart oracle, the 12-test Effect-disposal oracle, and the\n199-test Collection subscription-lifecycle oracle. The DB build, DB TypeScript\ncheck, targeted ESLint and Prettier checks, and `git diff --check` also passed.\n\nThe ancestor record\n[`issue-1891-async-cleanup.md`](issue-1891-async-cleanup.md) reviewed semantic\nhead `21ed924352e91e47f0643de6d2542051ddd12d20` and was versioned by\n`355554078675c9aaf924b649d2bdcd3636e40d4a`. Its receipts were 349 core tests,\n292 persistence tests with one existing todo, and 154 PowerSync tests, plus the\nstated type, lint, format, and build checks. That record classified pending late\nhooks and trigger disposal as an open design gap and named cleanup start only as\nthe destination. This follow-up implements and calibrates that destination; it\ndoes not rewrite the earlier verdict.\n\n## Design-grammar reconciliation\n\nReviewed semantic head:\n`20407a501a2441cd3780198809e75c00eb410925`\n\nThe frozen preservation contract required the model to retain cleanup start,\nall-owned retirement, observer and adapter failure aggregation, late-registration\nrollback, one notification per owner per boundary, terminal dependent ownership\nacross source restart, and the PowerSync pending-resource refinement. The\nsmallest reconstruction is a graph of independent laws rather than one model of\nevery lifecycle manager:\n\n- a three-cut timeline owns cleanup start, terminal `cleaned-up` publication,\n  and later public cleanup-Promise settlement;\n- observer registration ownership crosses cleanup-boundary identity, so one\n  registration receives one notification for each boundary until it\n  unsubscribes;\n- dependent ownership crosses sync-run identity, so source restart does not\n  revive a live query terminalized by the retired run;\n- setup rollback owns the inverted history in which a dependent registers while\n  cleanup is already active;\n- a provenance-labelled failure relation keeps adapter failure primary and\n  observer or local failure secondary; and\n- the PowerSync task collector refines the one adapter-cleanup obligation with\n  active, released, pending, and late hook cleanup plus present or in-flight\n  trigger disposal.\n\nThe core timeline now models all three observable cuts. At cleanup start,\nrestart admission is closed, public status is unchanged, and cleanup is\npending. At terminal publication, admission is open and status is `cleaned-up`\nwhile the original cleanup Promise remains pending. Promise settlement follows.\nThe model still does not reproduce callback registries, manager order, provider\ntasks, replay, or publication machinery.\n\n### Reconstruction, ablation, exclusion, and range\n\nEvery known valid witness reconstructs from the graph above. The timeline owns\nthe held-adapter case. Separate driver refinements cover live queries, Effects,\nrepeated aliases, late observers, source position, abort and release reentry,\nruntime cleanup return shape, concurrent callers, terminal-event restart, and\nfailure combinations. PowerSync separately covers each provider task origin and\ntheir consequential overlap.\n\nOne-at-a-time ablation produced these results:\n\n| Removed coordinate or relation | Lost distinction |\n| --- | --- |\n| Cleanup start | Dependents remain active while adapter cleanup is pending. |\n| Terminal-publication cut | Event-time restart and event-before-Promise order disappear. |\n| Cleanup-Promise cut | Adapter settlement becomes indistinguishable from public completion. |\n| Sync-run identity | A source restart may revive an already-terminal dependent. |\n| Registration by cleanup boundary | Once-per-boundary and once-per-lifetime become indistinguishable. |\n| Cleaning-source position | Setup can retain an owner acquired before the cleaning source. |\n| Adapter/local failure provenance | Rejection timing may choose the wrong primary failure. |\n| Runtime return shape | A contextual-`void` Promise can be mistaken for synchronous cleanup. |\n| PowerSync task origin | A late hook, released hook, or in-flight trigger can fall outside adapter ownership. |\n| PowerSync task-set growth | Cleanup can snapshot tasks once and settle before a late disposer. |\n\nThe two-attempt reentry coordinate does not justify a full Cartesian expansion\nby itself, but it retains one repeated-denial observation without meaningful\nruntime cost. The existing eight reentry cells therefore remain. Stable case\nIDs now calibrate the complete `abort|release × plain|nested × 1|2` product and\nthe complete `event|await × source|live-query` product. The executable check\nasserts the expected order, completeness, and uniqueness of all twelve IDs.\nThe source-position matrix and PowerSync result-shape matrices are explicit\none-dimensional enumerations with no derived product or skipped cells.\n\nNearby invalid states are rejected or kept outside this owner:\n\n- an observer registered after cleanup settlement does not retroactively\n  observe the old boundary;\n- restarting or recreating a live-query Collection differs from restarting\n  only its cleaned source;\n- a settled ordinary-unload failure is reported once rather than retroactively\n  adopted by a later cleanup; and\n- provider transport shutdown, demand replay, and general row publication\n  remain outside this oracle.\n\nNo independent marginal case was supplied after the grammar froze. Range is\ntherefore untested beyond the current core driver and the Node SQLite PowerSync\nrefinement. Existing persistence and PowerSync cases shaped the grammar and are\nnot held-out range evidence. Browser, mobile, multi-process, and provider\ntransport behavior remain outside the claim.\n\n### New executable controls\n\nThe core owner adds three focused witnesses:\n\n1. A cleanup-start observer throws before the adapter cleanup later rejects.\n   The final `AggregateError` keeps the wrapped adapter failure as `cause` and\n   the observer failure as its secondary error.\n2. One ordinary observer remains registered across two cleanup/restart\n   boundaries, receives exactly one notification for each, then receives none\n   after unsubscribe.\n3. Restarting a cleaned source leaves the original dependent live query in\n   terminal error, preserves its prior result, and emits no replacement\n   publication.\n\nThe PowerSync refinement adds the higher-order overlap omitted by the separate\nlate-hook and in-flight-trigger cases. One cleanup starts while trigger creation\nand another subset hook are both pending. Both later cleanup functions are\nowned and invoked once. Hook cleanup rejects first while trigger disposal stays\npending. Cleanup remains pending, then rejects with trigger disposal as the\ndeterministic primary failure, without duplicate adapter logging or an\nunhandled rejection.\n\nThe direct observer-plus-adapter checker was calibrated with a temporary\npriority-inversion mutant. The mutant made the observer failure the aggregate\ncause and failed the intended test at its exact cause assertion: one failure and\n33 skipped tests. Restoring production made all 34 core oracle tests pass. No\nproduction change was required.\n\n### Reusable boundary-law prompts\n\n| Prompt | Disposition |\n| --- | --- |\n| Real-provider conformance | Applicable to the provider refinement. PowerSync runs through `@powersync/node` and a real temporary Node SQLite database. The in-memory core fixture makes no provider-value claim. |\n| Minimal ambiguity | Applicable and covered. Incidental non-Promise return, runtime Promise, synchronous throw, asynchronous rejection, and fulfillment have separate witnesses. |\n| Name invariance | Not applicable. No user-controlled name or SQL alias selects cleanup classification. |\n| Representation symmetry | Applicable only to cleanup return representation. Function and object cleanup forms plus contextual-`void` runtime Promises preserve the same settlement law. |\n| Await-boundary transitions | Applicable and covered at cleanup start, terminal publication, adapter settlement, late hook return, trigger creation, and disposer settlement. |\n| Local/transport refinement | Boundary only. Core and Node SQLite share lifecycle settlement; provider transport termination remains explicitly unclaimed. |\n| Partial-construction cleanup | Applicable and covered for cleaning-source position, pending hook acquisition, trigger creation, and their combined overlap. This does not claim every provider construction step. |\n| Value-and-work refinement | Not applicable as a bounded-work law. Exact once counts protect ownership, not performance or transfer cardinality. |\n\n### Exact-head environment and receipts\n\nThe semantic head ran with Node `24.19.0`, pnpm `11.1.0`, Vitest `3.2.4`,\nTypeScript `5.9.3`, `@powersync/node` `0.20.0`, and Darwin `25.6.0` on arm64.\n\n- The cleanup/restart oracle passed 34 of 34 tests with no type errors.\n- The PowerSync load-hook suite passed 33 of 33 tests with no type errors.\n- The full PowerSync package passed 176 of 176 tests across 11 files with no\n  type errors.\n- Targeted ESLint and Prettier checks passed.\n- `git diff --check` passed.\n\nThe first full DB-package verification executed 6,406 assertions across 216\nfiles but the type checker rejected the event recorder's broad\n`CollectionStatus` annotation. The follow-up semantic head above narrows that\nobservation from the already-discriminated event value. The targeted oracle and\nits type check then passed. The final full DB-package rerun passed all 6,406\ntests across 216 files with no type errors.\n\n## Multiple cleanup-start failure reconciliation\n\nReviewed semantic head:\n`eb93f42094a365f5b2b599504ed4ffea4da66bbb`\n\nReview baseline: `7de64caeeb46c0a6d2b9f5c7e7cf424d7e6193da`\n\nA final current-head review found one failure-fidelity gap. Cleanup start\nattempted every observer, but `runAllCallbacks` rethrew only the first observer\nfailure and `beginCleanup` retained only one local failure. With two throwing\nobservers and a rejecting adapter cleanup, the public `AggregateError` retained\nthe wrapped adapter failure and the first observer failure but silently dropped\nthe second observer failure.\n\nThe fixed oracle is the smallest distinguishing history: two cleanup-start\nobservers throw distinct sentinel errors in registration order, adapter cleanup\nrejects after a held gate, and cleanup then admits a new sync run. The RED run\nreached both observers and failed because `AggregateError.errors` had two entries\ninstead of three. Production now records every local teardown failure in\nexecution order. Adapter cleanup remains the primary `cause`; a lone local\nfailure keeps its identity; multiple local-only failures use the first local\nfailure as their aggregate cause. Teardown, terminal publication, and later\nrestart still complete.\n\n| Requirement | Reconciliation outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | Pass. The existing cleanup contract and ORC-010 failure-fidelity law require each cleanup diagnostic to remain distinguishable. The test does not claim provider transport shutdown or full demand/replay coverage. |\n| ORC-002 | Pass. Expected error identity and order come from callback execution order and adapter-primary precedence, not the production aggregator. |\n| ORC-003 | Pass. The existing contract, timeline model, fixed failure history, public Collection driver, and exact aggregate comparison remain distinguishable. |\n| ORC-004 | Not applicable. This is one fixed controlled history, not a generated-grammar claim. |\n| ORC-005 | Pass. The test calls public preload, cleanup, and restart entry points; observer call order, exact error identities, status, and restart are observed. |\n| ORC-006 | Pass. Baseline production was the hostile wrong design. It reached both callbacks and failed only the missing-third-error assertion. |\n| ORC-007 | Not applicable. No important generated property changed. |\n| ORC-008 | Not applicable. No stateful reference model changed. |\n| ORC-009 | Pass. Cleanup start, adapter cleanup, local teardown, settlement, and restart retain their glossary meanings. |\n| ORC-010 | Pass. The aggregate keeps the adapter failure as `cause` and retains both observer failures in execution order while cleanup releases resources and permits restart. |\n| ORC-011 | Not applicable. The named defect is information loss in one concrete aggregator; no meaningfully independent semantic formulation is needed beyond exact sentinel identities and order. |\n\nORC-012 is satisfied for this repair by the requirement-by-requirement record\nabove. The focused RED run failed one of one selected tests with no type errors.\nAfter the repair, the complete cleanup/restart oracle passed 37 of 37 tests,\nand the five-suite lifecycle collateral passed 204 of 204 tests with no type\nerrors. Targeted ESLint, Prettier, and `git diff --check` passed.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Issue #1891 cleanup-start follow-up oracle review\n\nReviewed semantic head: `14b06080e0881f41b5363b8a4a3ffd0c3b3aed31`\n\nBase and prior review-record head:\n`355554078675c9aaf924b649d2bdcd3636e40d4a`\n\nInitial implementation and control head:\n`2fa7fada018613063c2f0d4a915f244ec68412d1`\n\nEvaluate Review fix head: `8f0481ad1e5d096a372da7df0002c8d4c2319c9d`\n\nScope: the cleanup-start boundary in core, dependent live-query and Effect\ninvalidation, and the PowerSync late on-demand hook and trigger-disposal repair.\nThe primary executable owner remains\n`packages/db/tests/collection-cleanup-restart-oracle.test.ts`. PowerSync's\n`packages/powersync-db-collection/tests/load-hooks.test.ts` is a real-provider\nrefinement. This documentation closeout does not change the reviewed semantic\ntree.\n\n## Authority, issue history, and selected contract\n\nIssue #1891 exposed an existing cleanup barrier rather than inventing a new\none. The API and documentation tell callers to await cleanup before starting a\nnew sync run. The minimal reproduction returned a held Promise from a cleanup\ncallback and observed public cleanup settle before that Promise continued. At\nthe time of the report, the callback type and Collection implementation treated\nthe cleanup as `void` and ignored the runtime Promise.\n\nThat defect matters even when no row assertion fails immediately. If an old and\nreplacement Collection use the same SQLite resource, old cleanup can affect the\nreplacement or persist deletes after replacement hydration. Without a cleanup\nsettlement barrier, an application needs a separate drain or cancellation\nbarrier before constructing the replacement.\n\nTwo contracts were possible:\n\n1. Await runtime Promise-like cleanup results before terminal cleanup.\n2. Define cleanup as initiation-only and expose a separate settlement hook.\n\nThe project selected the first contract. The initiation-only alternative would\nhave required explicit documentation and another public barrier; silently using\nthose semantics does not satisfy the existing cleanup and restart contract.\nIssue #1889 is adjacent but distinct. It concerns late startup `markReady` and\ninitial-query readiness. Issue #1891 concerns settlement and invalidation of an\nending sync run. Cleanup start is not startup readiness, a Collection status, or\nproof that adapter resources settled.\n\nThe earlier repair added awaitable cleanup and preserved these laws:\n\n- concurrent cleanup callers share one Promise;\n- adapter rejection ends the old run once, publishes `cleaned-up`, rejects all\n  waiters with the adapter error as `SyncCleanupError.cause`, and does not retry\n  the retired callback;\n- a later sync run remains admissible;\n- incidental non-Promise returns from contextual-`void` callbacks remain\n  synchronous;\n- runtime Promise-like returns are awaited even when the callback is statically\n  typed to return `void`; and\n- the terminal `cleaned-up` event admits restart before a continuation on the\n  public cleanup Promise runs.\n\nThe previous exact-head review left two PowerSync histories open: an\n`onLoadSubset` hook that returns its disposer after cleanup starts, and trigger\ndisposal created by that pending acquisition. Merely delaying terminal\n`cleaned-up` until those resources settled was rejected. In the staged-preload\ncounterexample, the dependent live query remained active, accepted the late\nbaseline, and fulfilled preload before source-cleanup publication. The durable\ndesign therefore needed an earlier invalidation boundary distinct from terminal\nstatus.\n\nAt the reviewed head, cleanup start synchronously closes restart admission,\nputs each dependent live query in terminal error once, marks dependent Effects\ndisposed, and detaches subscription demand. Throwing and reentrant observers do\nnot strand teardown or observer ownership. The source Collection keeps its\nprior public status while adapter cleanup is pending. PowerSync owns pending\non-demand loads, late hook cleanup, already released cleanup Promises, and\ntrigger disposal until they settle. A pending hook-acquisition failure\nparticipates in cleanup failure selection, while an expected transaction-abort\nconsequence does not replace the primary cleanup failure. Only after all owned\nwork settles does core publish `cleaned-up` and settle public cleanup.\n\n## Oracle responsibilities\n\n- **Contract:** the public cleanup/restart contract, project glossary, and\n  live-query architecture authorize the two boundaries and state their limits.\n- **Model:** `expectedCleanupBoundary` is an independent two-checkpoint\n  timeline. It predicts restart admission, source status, public cleanup\n  settlement, and a combined dependent-terminal observation.\n- **History grammar:** fixed histories hold adapter cleanup at cleanup start and\n  settlement. Adjacent bounded cases cover live queries, repeated aliases,\n  Effects with pending handlers, abort/release reentry, nested cleanup,\n  concurrent callers, throwing and late observer registration, fulfillment,\n  rejection, runtime return shape, late hook return or rejection, already\n  released cleanup, trigger creation and disposal, and combined cleanup\n  failures.\n- **Production driver:** core calls public Collection, live-query, and Effect\n  entry points. PowerSync uses `@powersync/node` with a real temporary Node\n  SQLite database, public source Collections, and dependent live queries.\n- **Refinement check:** held gates define the observation cuts. Tests compare\n  exact status, restart admission, Promise identity and settlement, exact error\n  causes and precedence, dependent state, subscriber and observer ownership,\n  disposer and report counts, publications, rows, adapter logs, and the process\n  unhandled-rejection channel.\n\nThe model's `dependent: terminal` field is an explicit abstraction. It combines\ntwo different public observations: live-query status `error` and\n`Effect.disposed === true`. It does not claim those dependents have the same\ncleanup machinery. Cleanup start maps to the internal synchronous callback\nboundary. Cleanup settlement maps to the later public cleanup Promise cut.\n\n## ORC-001 through ORC-011\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001: contract authority and limits | Pass. `Collection.cleanup()`, the await-before-restart documentation, the glossary's cleanup and cleanup-start terms, and the live-query architecture authorize the result. The exact held-Promise reproduction and same-SQLite replacement hazard establish why settlement matters. The owner remains partial: it does not establish full demand/replay histories, provider transport shutdown, wrapper-wide persistence, general row-publication laws, Effect-handler ordering beyond the pending-disposal case, or native hosts other than the tested Node SQLite path. |\n| ORC-002: independent judgment | Pass. The expected timeline comes from the approved cleanup contract and rejected staged-preload counterexample. `expectedCleanupBoundary` imports no lifecycle transition, callback registry, adapter collector, or Promise classifier. Held gates and sentinel errors compute observations independently of production's cleanup queues and task sets. |\n| ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. The five responsibilities are listed above. The executable owner keeps its contract, limits, pure model, bounded scenarios, public production drivers, recorders, and named cleanup-start and cleanup-settlement checks together. Its repeated-alias, pending-handler, throwing-observer, and late-registration refinements are explicit fixed cases outside the two-checkpoint model. PowerSync keeps provider-specific ownership and failure histories beside its real SQLite driver. |\n| ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls | Not applicable. This follow-up makes no generated-history or input-grammar coverage claim. Its scenario products are bounded enumerations, and the late-hook and trigger tests are fixed controlled schedules. Reconstruction, ablation, random range, and exclusion controls are therefore not triggered. |\n| ORC-005: production path and observation | Pass. Core reaches public `cleanup()`, `preload()`, `startSyncImmediate()`, live-query status, and Effect disposal. It also observes repeated aliases, pending handler settlement, cleanup-start observer counts, exact observer failures, teardown completion, and later restart. PowerSync reaches its public Collection adapter through a real `PowerSyncDatabase` using Node SQLite, a source Collection, and a dependent live query. Positive witnesses include entered hook gates, disposer call counts, source subscriber and observer counts, and trigger creation/disposal. The recorders retain early settlement, wrong status, duplicate terminal reports, wrong error cause or precedence, unexpected publication or adapter logging, and unhandled rejection at the held checkpoints. |\n| ORC-006: checker calibration | Pass. At the initial implementation head, C-CORE-LIVE-START left a held live query `loading` instead of `error` (1 assertion failure, 18 passes); M-CORE-EFFECT-START observed `active` instead of `terminal` (1 assertion failure, 19 skipped); M-PS-TRIGGER-AWAIT settled both disposer lanes early (2 assertion failures, 18 skipped); C-PS-LATE-HOOK settled both late-hook lanes early (2 assertion failures, 18 skipped); and C-PS-RELEASED-HOOK settled both released-hook lanes early (2 assertion failures, 20 skipped). Final review controls reproduced duplicate alias reporting (`expected 1`, received `2`), one leaked late Effect observer (`expected 0`, received `1`), and a throwing late observer poisoning the next cleanup. The final in-flight-disposer mutant invoked without awaiting its Promise; both lanes reached the disposer and then failed the pending-settlement assertion (2 failures, 25 skipped). Every temporary mutation and control was restored. None was a timeout, setup failure, or unreached path. |\n| ORC-007: fixed and random campaigns with direct replay | Not applicable. No important generated property, generator, seed, run budget, shrink path, or replay interface changed. The bounded and fixed controls do not claim random coverage. |\n| ORC-008: stateful-model minimality | Not applicable. `expectedCleanupBoundary` is stateless recomputation over a prior status and checkpoint. It does not introduce, remove, combine, or split mutable state in a stateful reference model. The distinct cleanup-start and cleanup-settlement inputs preserve the next-action distinction that restart admission exposes. |\n| ORC-009: vocabulary mapping | Pass. `cleanup start`, cleanup, restart, sync run, Collection status, settlement, demand, physical acquisition, and acquisition release retain their glossary meanings. The model-only `dependent: terminal` abstraction maps explicitly to live-query terminal error or Effect disposed. Cleanup start is an internal invalidation cut, not the terminal `cleaned-up` status; cleanup settlement is distinct from the terminal event's earlier restart-admission cut. PowerSync's cleanup collector and shared active trigger-disposal Promise express implementation ownership, not new public lifecycle states. |\n| ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass. The tests do not shrink or normalize failures. Exact sentinel identity, `SyncCleanupError.cause`, settlement order, raw publication arrays, adapter logs, and unhandled-rejection arrays preserve the violated law and checkpoint. Core releases held gates, completes teardown after an observer throws, and cleans dependents in `finally`. PowerSync attempts all owned work, deterministically prefers trigger-disposal failure over later collector inspection, adopts a hook-acquisition failure without double logging it, and does not promote expected `SyncTransactionAbortedError` collateral to the primary cleanup failure. `withTestCleanup` retains the primary harness failure as `AggregateError.cause` and keeps secondary cleanup errors distinguishable. The mutants were restored before green verification. |\n| ORC-011: independent second formulation | Pass. The named shared-fault hypothesis is that core or a wrapper starts cleanup but discards a returned Promise or late-owned task. Core's in-memory timeline, the prior persisted same-resource/retired-owner refinement, and PowerSync's real SQLite hook/trigger path use meaningfully different machinery. Repeated-alias live-query reporting, pending Effect-handler disposal, and throwing or reentrant observer registration independently test cleanup-start invalidation and ownership. These formulations share only the settlement, invalidation, once-only release, and primary-failure laws. They make no equivalence claim about row ordering, projection, duplicates, or nonempty results. The same-SQLite multi-process and mobile-host risks remain outside this review. |\n\nORC-012 is satisfied by this versioned record for semantic head\n`14b06080e0881f41b5363b8a4a3ffd0c3b3aed31`. It records every ORC-001 through\nORC-011 outcome and is linked from the coverage map. The initial\n`2fa7fada018613063c2f0d4a915f244ec68412d1` head remains only as provenance for\nthe controls and pre-fix review findings described below.\n\n## Calibration details and counterexamples\n\nAt the initial `2fa7fada` implementation head, the PowerSync trigger mutant\nchanged `disposeTrackingAfterAbort()` from an owned Promise in\n`Promise.allSettled([trackingDisposal, ...pendingLoads])` to fire-and-forget.\nBoth fulfillment and rejection lanes reached disposer invocation. While the\ndisposer gate remained held, the public cleanup Promise settled. The restored\nimplementation stayed pending, then fulfilled or rejected with the exact\nsentinel cause after release.\n\nThe initial late-hook control held `onLoadSubset` before it returned a cleanup\ncallback. Cleanup start had to make the dependent live query terminal\nimmediately while the source status stayed `ready`. Releasing the hook installed\nits disposer without creating a trigger. Public cleanup remained pending until\nthat disposer settled. The rejection lane produced one `SyncCleanupError` with\nthe sentinel cause and no unhandled rejection. Rows and publication arrays\nstayed empty.\n\nThe already released hook control retired the live query first. Its held hook\ncleanup was no longer in the active demand map when Collection cleanup began.\nThe restored adapter adopted that Promise into the cleanup collector. Both\nlanes remained pending, invoked the hook once, and produced no unhandled\nrejection. This distinguishes ownership from mere presence in the current\ndemand map.\n\nThe initial core live-query control kept only the terminal `cleaned-up`\nlistener. With source cleanup held, the exact cleanup-start checkpoint observed\nlive-query status `loading` instead of `error`. This is the same missing\ndistinction that made the staged-preload counterexample possible.\n\nFinal core review reproduced three ownership and once-only failures before the\nfixes in `8f0481ad`:\n\n- two lexical aliases for one source emitted two terminal error reports instead\n  of one;\n- an Effect registered during active cleanup disposed immediately but retained\n  one cleanup-start observer; and\n- an immediately throwing late observer remained registered and rejected the\n  next cleanup.\n\nThe repaired lifecycle removes a late observer when its immediate registration\ncallback throws. Effect registration releases the callback when cleanup becomes\nactive before the unsubscribe handle can be retained. A fatal live query now\nignores later alias notifications. Focused coverage also holds an Effect handler\nopen while cleanup start disposes the Effect and releases its source\nsubscription, and proves that a throwing observer still allows adapter teardown,\nterminal publication, guard release, and later restart. Omitting cleanup-start\ndelivery from the final core tree produced three intended assertion failures.\n\nFinal PowerSync review exposed two more failure-loss paths. First,\nreconciliation could claim an in-flight trigger disposer after cleanup took its\nsnapshot. The cleanup-owned call then saw no published disposer and settled\nwithout joining the invocation already in progress. The shared\n`activeTrackingDisposal` Promise makes both owners join the same settlement.\nThe fulfillment and rejection lanes remain pending through disposer invocation;\na mutant that invoked but did not await the disposer failed both pending checks.\n\nSecond, rejection directly from a pending `onLoadSubset` hook was awaited but\ndiscarded. The active cleanup collector now retains that failure. The first\nbroader repair treated every captured pending-load rejection as a cleanup\nfailure, but the staged-baseline collateral test showed that this incorrectly\npromoted an expected `SyncTransactionAbortedError`. The final repair records the\nhook-acquisition failure at its boundary and leaves expected transaction-abort\ncollateral secondary. A combined probe rejects hook cleanup first and trigger\ndisposal later: cleanup waits for both, deterministically reports the trigger\nerror, emits no duplicate adapter log, and creates no unhandled rejection.\nOrdinary unload remains synchronous and reports a later unadopted hook failure\nexactly once.\n\nTwo earlier controls remain independent evidence rather than final-head\nmutants. The prior R4 ordering mutant produced\n`adapter cleanup -> public Promise continuation -> cleaned-up event`; the core\noracle rejected it because the terminal event must precede the continuation.\nThe terminal-only delay candidate awaited late PowerSync resources but left the\ndependent live query active. Its staged preload fulfilled before cleanup\npublication; the expected outcome was rejection at cleanup start. The assertion\nfailure removed that candidate in favor of cleanup start.\n\n## Verification and prior reviewed-head receipts\n\nAt the reviewed head:\n\n- the cleanup/restart oracle passed 24 of 24 tests;\n- its TypeScript check passed; and\n- `git diff --check` passed before this documentation closeout.\n\nAt the Evaluate Review fix head `8f0481ad`, before the final behavior-preserving\ndeduplication simplification:\n\n- the Effect collateral suite passed 86 of 86 tests;\n- the Collection subscription-lifecycle collateral passed 445 of 445 tests;\n- DB TypeScript and targeted ESLint checks passed;\n- the full PowerSync package passed 170 of 170 tests across 11 files with no\n  type errors;\n- the PowerSync production build and targeted ESLint check passed; and\n- the final no-await in-flight trigger-disposal mutant failed both intended\n  assertions and was restored.\n\nThe initial `2fa7fada` implementation receipts were 32 of 32 focused core and\nEffect tests, 22 of 22 focused PowerSync load-hook tests, and 165 of 165 full\nPowerSync tests across 11 files, plus the stated DB, DB IVM, PowerSync, lint,\nformat, and diff checks. One earlier PowerSync package run at that stage raced\nits build and could not resolve `dist/esm/index.d.ts`; that was a setup-order\nfailure, and the post-build 165-test rerun was green.\n\n## Late live-query registration reconciliation\n\nReviewed semantic head:\n`889b930170c7c23b75f020297d96fa92f3c31142`\n\nReview baseline: `9cc05782a035ca737e2c3dc360ae4ed417be2045`\n\nA later review found that a live query could start while one source's adapter\ncleanup was pending. Cleanup-start observer registration correctly delivered\nthe active boundary synchronously and put the live query in terminal error, but\nthe builder continued its setup loop. At the baseline, the controlled\nfirst-source history observed one subscription on both the cleaning source and\nthe later healthy source. An intermediate guard stopped later acquisition but\nleft one earlier healthy-source subscription when the cleaning source occupied\nthe second position. The final repair throws the exact terminal setup error\nafter unregistering the current observers. The existing sync-entry rollback\nthen releases all earlier source ownership and prevents later acquisition.\n\nThe coverage gap was an inverted lifecycle history. Existing tests created a\ndependent before cleanup began or registered an Effect during active cleanup.\nThey did not start a live query after cleanup start, nor vary the cleaning\nsource's position in a multi-source query. The permanent two-cell refinement\nholds adapter cleanup, places the cleaning source first and second, calls the\npublic live-query preload path, and checks the exact terminal error, one report,\nand zero retained subscriptions on both sources.\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001: contract authority and limits | Pass. The glossary and live-query architecture require cleanup start to put dependent live queries in terminal error. This focused refinement adds the setup-order consequence: setup that observes that terminal boundary retains no partial source ownership. It does not expand the oracle's transport, row-publication, or full demand-history claims. |\n| ORC-002: independent judgment | Pass. A held adapter-cleanup gate creates the boundary. Exact public status, error text, report count, and source subscriber counts supply the expected observations without importing builder state or transition helpers. |\n| ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. The existing oracle opening retains its contract, two-checkpoint model, fixed history grammar, public driver, and refinement checks. The new fixed source-position matrix is named as an adjacent ownership refinement outside the small timeline model. |\n| ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls | Not applicable. The first/second source product is a bounded two-cell enumeration, not a generated-history coverage claim. |\n| ORC-005: production path and observation | Pass. The driver calls real source `preload()` and `cleanup()`, creates a real joined live-query Collection, and calls its public `preload()`. Both cells reach synchronous cleanup-start delivery while adapter cleanup remains held. The checkpoint observes terminal status and exact subscriber ownership before cleanup settlement. |\n| ORC-006: checker calibration | Pass. At the baseline, the first-source cell failed with `{ cleaning: 1, healthy: 1 }` instead of zeros. The intermediate stop-only guard made that cell pass but the second-source cell failed with `{ cleaning: 0, healthy: 1 }`. Neither result was a timeout, setup failure, or unreached path. The final rollback repair passes both cells. |\n| ORC-007: fixed and random campaigns with direct replay | Not applicable. No important generated property, seed, budget, shrink path, or replay interface changed. |\n| ORC-008: stateful-model minimality | Not applicable. No stateful reference-model state was added, removed, combined, or split. Source position is a fixed driver dimension. |\n| ORC-009: vocabulary mapping | Pass. Cleanup start, cleanup settlement, live-query Collection, source Collection, subscription, and sync run retain their glossary meanings. Partial source ownership means subscriptions acquired before terminal setup rollback; it is an observation, not a new lifecycle state. |\n| ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass. The test preserves exact status, error, log, and subscriber-count observations. `finally` releases the adapter gate, cleans the live query and both sources, and cannot replace the primary assertion failure. |\n| ORC-011: independent second formulation | Pass. The existing late-Effect-registration refinement independently proves immediate disposal and observer release. The new live-query path instead exercises joined-source setup rollback and subscription ownership. Both refine cleanup-start invalidation without equating their teardown machinery. |\n\nORC-012 is satisfied by this versioned section for semantic head\n`889b930170c7c23b75f020297d96fa92f3c31142`. Focused verification passed the\n30-test cleanup/restart oracle, the 12-test Effect-disposal oracle, and the\n199-test Collection subscription-lifecycle oracle. The DB build, DB TypeScript\ncheck, targeted ESLint and Prettier checks, and `git diff --check` also passed.\n\nThe ancestor record\n[`issue-1891-async-cleanup.md`](issue-1891-async-cleanup.md) reviewed semantic\nhead `21ed924352e91e47f0643de6d2542051ddd12d20` and was versioned by\n`355554078675c9aaf924b649d2bdcd3636e40d4a`. Its receipts were 349 core tests,\n292 persistence tests with one existing todo, and 154 PowerSync tests, plus the\nstated type, lint, format, and build checks. That record classified pending late\nhooks and trigger disposal as an open design gap and named cleanup start only as\nthe destination. This follow-up implements and calibrates that destination; it\ndoes not rewrite the earlier verdict.\n\n## Design-grammar reconciliation\n\nReviewed semantic head:\n`20407a501a2441cd3780198809e75c00eb410925`\n\nThe frozen preservation contract required the model to retain cleanup start,\nall-owned retirement, observer and adapter failure aggregation, late-registration\nrollback, one notification per owner per boundary, terminal dependent ownership\nacross source restart, and the PowerSync pending-resource refinement. The\nsmallest reconstruction is a graph of independent laws rather than one model of\nevery lifecycle manager:\n\n- a three-cut timeline owns cleanup start, terminal `cleaned-up` publication,\n  and later public cleanup-Promise settlement;\n- observer registration ownership crosses cleanup-boundary identity, so one\n  registration receives one notification for each boundary until it\n  unsubscribes;\n- dependent ownership crosses sync-run identity, so source restart does not\n  revive a live query terminalized by the retired run;\n- setup rollback owns the inverted history in which a dependent registers while\n  cleanup is already active;\n- a provenance-labelled failure relation keeps adapter failure primary and\n  observer or local failure secondary; and\n- the PowerSync task collector refines the one adapter-cleanup obligation with\n  active, released, pending, and late hook cleanup plus present or in-flight\n  trigger disposal.\n\nThe core timeline now models all three observable cuts. At cleanup start,\nrestart admission is closed, public status is unchanged, and cleanup is\npending. At terminal publication, admission is open and status is `cleaned-up`\nwhile the original cleanup Promise remains pending. Promise settlement follows.\nThe model still does not reproduce callback registries, manager order, provider\ntasks, replay, or publication machinery.\n\n### Reconstruction, ablation, exclusion, and range\n\nEvery known valid witness reconstructs from the graph above. The timeline owns\nthe held-adapter case. Separate driver refinements cover live queries, Effects,\nrepeated aliases, late observers, source position, abort and release reentry,\nruntime cleanup return shape, concurrent callers, terminal-event restart, and\nfailure combinations. PowerSync separately covers each provider task origin and\ntheir consequential overlap.\n\nOne-at-a-time ablation produced these results:\n\n| Removed coordinate or relation | Lost distinction |\n| --- | --- |\n| Cleanup start | Dependents remain active while adapter cleanup is pending. |\n| Terminal-publication cut | Event-time restart and event-before-Promise order disappear. |\n| Cleanup-Promise cut | Adapter settlement becomes indistinguishable from public completion. |\n| Sync-run identity | A source restart may revive an already-terminal dependent. |\n| Registration by cleanup boundary | Once-per-boundary and once-per-lifetime become indistinguishable. |\n| Cleaning-source position | Setup can retain an owner acquired before the cleaning source. |\n| Adapter/local failure provenance | Rejection timing may choose the wrong primary failure. |\n| Runtime return shape | A contextual-`void` Promise can be mistaken for synchronous cleanup. |\n| PowerSync task origin | A late hook, released hook, or in-flight trigger can fall outside adapter ownership. |\n| PowerSync task-set growth | Cleanup can snapshot tasks once and settle before a late disposer. |\n\nThe two-attempt reentry coordinate does not justify a full Cartesian expansion\nby itself, but it retains one repeated-denial observation without meaningful\nruntime cost. The existing eight reentry cells therefore remain. Stable case\nIDs now calibrate the complete `abort|release × plain|nested × 1|2` product and\nthe complete `event|await × source|live-query` product. The executable check\nasserts the expected order, completeness, and uniqueness of all twelve IDs.\nThe source-position matrix and PowerSync result-shape matrices are explicit\none-dimensional enumerations with no derived product or skipped cells.\n\nNearby invalid states are rejected or kept outside this owner:\n\n- an observer registered after cleanup settlement does not retroactively\n  observe the old boundary;\n- restarting or recreating a live-query Collection differs from restarting\n  only its cleaned source;\n- a settled ordinary-unload failure is reported once rather than retroactively\n  adopted by a later cleanup; and\n- provider transport shutdown, demand replay, and general row publication\n  remain outside this oracle.\n\nNo independent marginal case was supplied after the grammar froze. Range is\ntherefore untested beyond the current core driver and the Node SQLite PowerSync\nrefinement. Existing persistence and PowerSync cases shaped the grammar and are\nnot held-out range evidence. Browser, mobile, multi-process, and provider\ntransport behavior remain outside the claim.\n\n### New executable controls\n\nThe core owner adds three focused witnesses:\n\n1. A cleanup-start observer throws before the adapter cleanup later rejects.\n   The final `AggregateError` keeps the wrapped adapter failure as `cause` and\n   the observer failure as its secondary error.\n2. One ordinary observer remains registered across two cleanup/restart\n   boundaries, receives exactly one notification for each, then receives none\n   after unsubscribe.\n3. Restarting a cleaned source leaves the original dependent live query in\n   terminal error, preserves its prior result, and emits no replacement\n   publication.\n\nThe PowerSync refinement adds the higher-order overlap omitted by the separate\nlate-hook and in-flight-trigger cases. One cleanup starts while trigger creation\nand another subset hook are both pending. Both later cleanup functions are\nowned and invoked once. Hook cleanup rejects first while trigger disposal stays\npending. Cleanup remains pending, then rejects with trigger disposal as the\ndeterministic primary failure, without duplicate adapter logging or an\nunhandled rejection.\n\nThe direct observer-plus-adapter checker was calibrated with a temporary\npriority-inversion mutant. The mutant made the observer failure the aggregate\ncause and failed the intended test at its exact cause assertion: one failure and\n33 skipped tests. Restoring production made all 34 core oracle tests pass. No\nproduction change was required.\n\n### Reusable boundary-law prompts\n\n| Prompt | Disposition |\n| --- | --- |\n| Real-provider conformance | Applicable to the provider refinement. PowerSync runs through `@powersync/node` and a real temporary Node SQLite database. The in-memory core fixture makes no provider-value claim. |\n| Minimal ambiguity | Applicable and covered. Incidental non-Promise return, runtime Promise, synchronous throw, asynchronous rejection, and fulfillment have separate witnesses. |\n| Name invariance | Not applicable. No user-controlled name or SQL alias selects cleanup classification. |\n| Representation symmetry | Applicable only to cleanup return representation. Function and object cleanup forms plus contextual-`void` runtime Promises preserve the same settlement law. |\n| Await-boundary transitions | Applicable and covered at cleanup start, terminal publication, adapter settlement, late hook return, trigger creation, and disposer settlement. |\n| Local/transport refinement | Boundary only. Core and Node SQLite share lifecycle settlement; provider transport termination remains explicitly unclaimed. |\n| Partial-construction cleanup | Applicable and covered for cleaning-source position, pending hook acquisition, trigger creation, and their combined overlap. This does not claim every provider construction step. |\n| Value-and-work refinement | Not applicable as a bounded-work law. Exact once counts protect ownership, not performance or transfer cardinality. |\n\n### Exact-head environment and receipts\n\nThe semantic head ran with Node `24.19.0`, pnpm `11.1.0`, Vitest `3.2.4`,\nTypeScript `5.9.3`, `@powersync/node` `0.20.0`, and Darwin `25.6.0` on arm64.\n\n- The cleanup/restart oracle passed 34 of 34 tests with no type errors.\n- The PowerSync load-hook suite passed 33 of 33 tests with no type errors.\n- The full PowerSync package passed 176 of 176 tests across 11 files with no\n  type errors.\n- Targeted ESLint and Prettier checks passed.\n- `git diff --check` passed.\n\nThe first full DB-package verification executed 6,406 assertions across 216\nfiles but the type checker rejected the event recorder's broad\n`CollectionStatus` annotation. The follow-up semantic head above narrows that\nobservation from the already-discriminated event value. The targeted oracle and\nits type check then passed. The final full DB-package rerun passed all 6,406\ntests across 216 files with no type errors.\n\n## Multiple cleanup-start failure reconciliation\n\nReviewed semantic head:\n`eb93f42094a365f5b2b599504ed4ffea4da66bbb`\n\nReview baseline: `7de64caeeb46c0a6d2b9f5c7e7cf424d7e6193da`\n\nA final current-head review found one failure-fidelity gap. Cleanup start\nattempted every observer, but `runAllCallbacks` rethrew only the first observer\nfailure and `beginCleanup` retained only one local failure. With two throwing\nobservers and a rejecting adapter cleanup, the public `AggregateError` retained\nthe wrapped adapter failure and the first observer failure but silently dropped\nthe second observer failure.\n\nThe fixed oracle is the smallest distinguishing history: two cleanup-start\nobservers throw distinct sentinel errors in registration order, adapter cleanup\nrejects after a held gate, and cleanup then admits a new sync run. The RED run\nreached both observers and failed because `AggregateError.errors` had two entries\ninstead of three. Production now records every local teardown failure in\nexecution order. Adapter cleanup remains the primary `cause`; a lone local\nfailure keeps its identity; multiple local-only failures use the first local\nfailure as their aggregate cause. Teardown, terminal publication, and later\nrestart still complete.\n\n| Requirement | Reconciliation outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | Pass. The existing cleanup contract and ORC-010 failure-fidelity law require each cleanup diagnostic to remain distinguishable. The test does not claim provider transport shutdown or full demand/replay coverage. |\n| ORC-002 | Pass. Expected error identity and order come from callback execution order and adapter-primary precedence, not the production aggregator. |\n| ORC-003 | Pass. The existing contract, timeline model, fixed failure history, public Collection driver, and exact aggregate comparison remain distinguishable. |\n| ORC-004 | Not applicable. This is one fixed controlled history, not a generated-grammar claim. |\n| ORC-005 | Pass. The test calls public preload, cleanup, and restart entry points; observer call order, exact error identities, status, and restart are observed. |\n| ORC-006 | Pass. Baseline production was the hostile wrong design. It reached both callbacks and failed only the missing-third-error assertion. |\n| ORC-007 | Not applicable. No important generated property changed. |\n| ORC-008 | Not applicable. No stateful reference model changed. |\n| ORC-009 | Pass. Cleanup start, adapter cleanup, local teardown, settlement, and restart retain their glossary meanings. |\n| ORC-010 | Pass. The aggregate keeps the adapter failure as `cause` and retains both observer failures in execution order while cleanup releases resources and permits restart. |\n| ORC-011 | Not applicable. The named defect is information loss in one concrete aggregator; no meaningfully independent semantic formulation is needed beyond exact sentinel identities and order. |\n\nORC-012 is satisfied for this repair by the requirement-by-requirement record\nabove. The focused RED run failed one of one selected tests with no type errors.\nAfter the repair, the complete cleanup/restart oracle passed 37 of 37 tests,\nand the five-suite lifecycle collateral passed 204 of 204 tests with no type\nerrors. Targeted ESLint, Prettier, and `git diff --check` passed.\n"}
